\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\title{How to read these notes}
\date{2026-11-07}

\p{The reading route has 14 parts. The diagrams show selected
prerequisites for reading, not every premise of every result. An arrow
means that its destination uses a construction or comparison from its
source. Each statement still gives its precise hypotheses. Questions on
the arrows motivate the next topic; they do not assert that the notes
already derive the corresponding physics.}

\p{The overview includes the full intended route. The
[prime-by-prime Gaussian extension](fdip-008M),
[Structure](fdip-008R) and the concluding
[comparison of fixed and free data](fdip-009G) are published.
The [Grassmann integration chapter](fdip-008E),
[fermionic elimination](fdip-009I),
[covariant Dirac operators](fdip-009N) and the
[Wilson term](fdip-009T) develop the fermion
part. The [compatibility test](fdip-009Y) fixes one finite
spinor datum. The [larger proposals](fdip-00A2) remain open tests.
A solid arrow into open material records an intended prerequisite,
not a result already proved. In the part diagrams, a dotted box names a
neighboring part; every arrow is solid.}

