\p{After backgrounds and symmetry, the route separates finite-group
sectors from Clifford algebras and spatial Dirac operators. The latter
lead to arithmetic, approximation on a supplied circle and geometric
realization. These are not three independent branches: the
[fiber-constancy criterion](fdip-0042) remains in Arithmetic and is used
by Realization. The direct comparison from Part V also remains visible.
The figures therefore show selected prerequisites, not an exact
transitive reduction of all dependencies.}

\p{Finite-group sectors, circle approximation and realization meet in
the study of selected backgrounds and induced actions. Connections lead
to gauge fields; Euclidean time and the fermion branch then feed
the Structure part. The fermion branch currently supplies its
Grassmann integration, elimination, covariant Dirac operators and the
free Wilson remedy. The arrow from gauge fields to Euclidean time
meets the [gauge transfer](fdip-007Y) and [Gauss's law](fdip-0088).
Reflection positivity alone does not supply that shift. The arrow
from gauge fields to lattice fermions meets the covariant Dirac
operator and the free Wilson remedy above, not Grassmann
integration or fermionic elimination, and it does not identify
those operators with a physical electron.
Arithmetic and Structure contribute different
questions to the closing explorations. These destinations are earned by
specific finite constructions, rather than adopted as starting axioms.}

\ul{
  \li{Part I, [Foundations](fdip-0015): fields on finite carriers,
  reference pairings, differences and algebraic elimination.}
  \li{Part II, [Gaussian laws](fdip-002B): states, reference measure,
  positive and constrained Gaussian laws, and retained observables.}
  \li{Part III, [Backgrounds and symmetry](fdip-0019): transported
  data, observables and half-turn sectors.}
  \li{Part IV, [Finite-group symmetry](fdip-004R): real isotypic
  projectors, multiplicities and Gaussian sector laws.}
  \li{Part V, [Clifford algebras](fdip-001B),
  [spatial Dirac operators](fdip-001C) and
  [enriched comparison](fdip-001E): distinct questions about quadratic
  forms, first-order operators and preserved observables.}
  \li{Part VI, [Arithmetic families](fdip-001D), followed by the
  [Gaussian extension at every prime](fdip-008M).}
  \li{Part VII, [Continuum approximation](fdip-004F): a supplied
  circle and fixed Fourier modes, not a general emergent continuum.}
  \li{Part VIII, [Geometric realization](fdip-004L): which information
  survives a replacement by a shape, including the weights it can forget.}
  \li{Part IX, [Selected backgrounds](fdip-0059),
  [induced actions and interactions](fdip-005Q),
  [local laws and gluing](fdip-0063), and
  [connections and holonomy](fdip-006C).}
  \li{Part X, [Gauge fields](fdip-006L): finite surface laws,
  matter-induced actions, dynamical connections and compact groups.}
  \li{Part XI, [Euclidean time](fdip-007L): reflected pairings,
  explicit transfer operators and Gauss's law. Reflection positivity
  alone does not supply a time shift.}
  \li{Part XII, [Lattice fermions](fdip-008E): finite Grassmann
  integration, determinants, Pfaffians, Wick identities and
  [elimination](fdip-009I), followed by
  [covariant Dirac operators and doubling](fdip-009N) and the
  [Wilson remedy](fdip-009T).}
  \li{Part XIII, [Structure](fdip-008R): operator-algebra,
  cochain, spectral-geometry, coarse-graining and gluing questions,
  developed in the chapters of that part.
  Speculative and optional sections retain those qualifications.}
  \li{Part XIV, [Explorations and synthesis](fdip-003W): the existing
  conjecture and finite interfaces, the
  [compatibility test](fdip-009Y), and a comparison of what is fixed
  or free. The [larger proposals](fdip-00A2) remain open tests.
  The [synthesis](fdip-003W) records the remaining open comparisons:
  the physical environment map, the continuum descriptions
  constructed from finite data in [[fdip-00AV]], with no moduli
  map constructed, and geometric or moduli
  structure beyond the published groupoid and realization.}
}

\p{The part diagrams appear at their openings. The Structure diagram
routes its time input to the transfer chapter and its fermion input to
the Dirac-Kähler chapter, where those suppliers are actually intended.
It does not make the earlier states or cochains definitions consequences
of time evolution or fermions. The root retains one linear reading order;
forward examples and open questions orient that reading without becoming
premises of the preceding proofs.}
